{
  "title": "Claw Toe",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/foot-and-ankle/7013/claw-toe",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jan 23 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Claw toe is a lesser toe deformity characterized by MTP hyperextension and resulting PIP and DIP flexion.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with presence of MTP hyperextension, PIP flexion and DIP flexion of a lesser toe.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is a trial of nonoperative management with shoe modification and taping. Surgical management is indicated for progressive deformity, fixed contractures, and dorsal toe ulcerations.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "typically involves multiple toes",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "often bilateral",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "MTP hyperextension is the primary pathology",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "chronic MTP hyperextension leads to unopposed flexion of the DIP and PIP by FDL",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "analogous to intrinsic minus deformity in the hand",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "the MTP **plantar plate becomes insufficient** over time",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**base of proximal phalanx translates dorsally**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**interossei and lumbricals move dorsally**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "shifts flexion moment arm to the wrong side of the center of rotation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cause",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**synovitis** is the most common cause",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "trauma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "delayed or missed **compartment syndrome** involving the deep posterior compartment of the leg or foot",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "cavus deformity",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**neuromuscular disease** affecting intrinsic and extrinsic muscle balance",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "clawing of all 4 lesser toes implicates a neurologic abnormality",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**inflammatory arthropathies**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "lead to soft tissue structure attenuation and MTP joint instability",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
